R. David Murray writes:
>I solve the AC problem for the laptop by using a Poqet, which runs 
>on AA batteries.  At the moment the only modems I have do require AC, so I 
>have to check into that, but I'm thinking about picking up a WorldPort or 
>some such.  Anyone have any recomendations in that area?  Is there such a 
>beast that comes with one of those green stickers?

Auto-transformers which convert between US and UK voltages can be used to
power the modems and/or laptop battery charger mains power units.  In the
UK, Maplin Electronics stores supply a 100VA transformer (should be ample
wattage to power modems, etc) for the equivalent of around $37.  This type
comes complete with a UK mains lead and standard American 3-pin outlet
socket in the cover.  The cheaper model where you have to supply the leads
is $22.  These must be readily available from US electrical stores, and a
plastic lunch box could be used as the device's cabinet.

Modems which are BABT-approved include US Robotics/Miracom, Zoom, Pace,
Hayes, and Dataflex.  There is a popular British comedy series which
features an entrepreneurial character who sometimes falls foul of the law.
A few years ago, one episode had him supplying telephones which were not
BABT-approved.  So he went into British Telecom showrooms with his
assistants, acted as if they were viewing BT's range of phones, and whilst
no one was looking, peeled off as many green stickers as they could get
away with to fit onto his own stock of phones.

I'm not sure about compatibility of modem <---> telephone socket leads, 
apparently they're different in some countries.   

If all else fails and any visiting Extropians' laptops can take an internal
modem card and 1200 bps is acceptable, I have a spare BABT-approved modem
complete with lead which I could lend out.  Rather slow for international
calls though!

