From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 24 May 94 14:08:40 EDT
Subject: [#94-5-273] AI: NeuronicComputer/Microtubules/Hameroff

QUERY:  I am looking for any reference material, citations and opinions about
neuronic microtubules as a means of computing information in our brains.

I have seen references to Stuart Hameroff's (Univ. of Arizona) work in David
Freedman's *Brainmakers* talking about a paradigm shift in the AI community.
Possibly Roger Penrose is also looking at microtubules from a QM viewpoint,
Discover magazine?

Apparently, from what I've heard from others, but haven't read myself: Hameroff
suggests that the microtubule network in neurons actually functions as a
naturally evolved computer.  He thinks these microtubule computers, if they
exist, function similarly to Von Neumann-type cellular automatons. If these
microtubule computers exist inside nerve cells, then, he says, they increase the
complexity of modeling the brain a billion fold.  Microtubules can be coaxed to
self-organize into networks and once he figures out how, suggests using them to
build nanocomputers for cell repair machines.

I have read an interesting reference to microtubules and spontaneous order at
the cellular level from "Science"  8 April 1994: 223.  Hess, B. & Mikhailov, A.
"Self-Organization in Living Cells."

"At such smaller scales, the chemical system of a living cell is far from
resembling a macroscopic unstirred chemical reactor.  Rather, it should be
viewed as a network formed by a population of active macromolecules that is
characterized by a very high degree of 'communication' between its members.
This implies a different mode of self-organization similar to the collective
behavior of insect societies or immune networks.  Another special aspect of
self-organization in living cells is the abundance of energy contained in
thermal fluctuations.  These strong thermal fluctuations can be employed by
far-from-equilibrium subsystems inside the cells."

"The laws of thermodynamics prevent the directed use of thermal fluctuations.
However, as shown by Feynman is his analysis of the thermal ratchet (a process
allowing motion in one direction only), this does not generally apply to systems
with some components that are far from thermal equilibrium (and thus, 'Maxwell's
demon' may operate if it receives and dissipates energy from external sources).
It might be that the ratchet effects are involved to a much larger extent in the
function of living cells and the organization of intracellular traffic.  The
temporal self-organization of chemical processes, expressed in the generation of
different periodicities and interactions between them, plays a fundamental role
in living cells.  In this framework, the dynamic processes in larger cellular
structures such as genomic components or the cellular actin and tubulin, the
shape producing and controlling entities, should not be overlooked.  The dynamic
network of microtubules plays an essential role in the self-organization of
cellular structures, turnover, and motility."

Thanks for any help and/or opinions in advance.

